{"question_id": "95228167", "hypothesis": "Nice \u2014 fun trip ahead. Here\u2019s a practical checklist you can use in the store, plus a few tips tied to things you\u2019ve mentioned (Les Paul vs Strat, open D, coil-splitting, fingerpicking).\n\nQuick prep\n- Bring a tuner and a strap (or ask to use the store\u2019s).  \n- Bring a couple of your favorite picks and your phone to record sound or take photos/serial numbers.  \n- Know your budget and whether you want new or used.\n\nWhat to play and how to test\n- Play the same simple riffs and chords on every guitar so you can compare feel and tone. Include something you fingerpick (e.g., the Blackbird-style pattern) and a chunky rhythm so you hear split-pickup behavior.  \n- Try each guitar both plugged in and unplugged. Unplugged resonance gives a lot of info for fingerpicking/acoustic-like tone.  \n- If you plan to use open D often, ask to tune one guitar to open D (store may oblige) so you can test string tension, chord voicings and resonance in that tuning.\n\nFeel & playability\n- Check neck shape and thickness (comfort while fretting, thumb position).  \n- Check action (string height) and fret access/feel \u2014 play high up the neck to check for buzzing or dead spots.  \n- Test playability sitting and standing (weight distribution matters\u2014Les Pauls are heavier).  \n- Try different string gauges if possible; lighter gauges help fingerpicking but change tone.\n\nSetup, frets & intonation\n- Check intonation across the neck: play open string, then 12th-fret harmonic and fretted 12th note.  \n- Look for fret buzz or dead frets, and check that frets are level.  \n- Check truss rod access and range (ask if it\u2019s been recently set up).  \n- Ask when it was last set up (or plan to budget for a professional setup).\n\nPickups & electronics\n- Compare pickup voicings: humbuckers (Les Paul) vs single-coils (Strat) \u2014 warm and thick vs bright and snappy.  \n- If you want versatility, try guitars with coil-splitting/coiltap to get single-coil-ish tones from humbuckers. Test the split in both neck and bridge positions.  \n- Turn knobs and flip switches while amplified to check for crackling/pots noise and switch reliability.  \n- Listen for microphonics or unwanted noise at different volumes and pickup settings.\n\nHardware & construction\n- Check tuners for smoothness and stability.  \n- Check bridge and saddle condition; tremolo systems should feel stable if present.  \n- Inspect finish for cracks, dents, or patches (especially on used guitars).  \n- On Les Pauls, check weight and balance (neck dive, how it sits on a strap).\n\nSound through an amp\n- Play through an amp you\u2019ll use or something representative of your style. Try clean and a couple of overdrive settings\u2014listen for clarity and note separation.  \n- If you use effects, test with them as well (reverb, delay, compression, etc.). Record a short clip if allowed so you can listen later.\n\nPractical stuff & policy\n- Ask about warranty, return policy, and setup included (some stores do a basic setup).  \n- For used instruments, ask about history/repairs and check serial numbers.  \n- Check case or gig bag condition and whether it\u2019s included.\n\nFinal tips\n- Trust your hands: comfort and inspiration > specs.  \n- Compare several guitars in the same price range \u2014 small differences can become big in feel/tone.  \n- If torn between two, sleep on it and listen to your recordings the next day.\n\nIf you want, tell me your budget and the models you\u2019ll try and I\u2019ll help narrow the choice or make a short scorecard you can use in the store.", "autoeval_label": {"model": "gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": false}}
{"question_id": "505af2f5", "hypothesis": "Nice \u2014 since you already make almond milk + vanilla + honey, I\u2019ll build on that and give a few healthier, low\u2011sugar options (vegan and dairy) plus tips to make them creamy and shelf\u2011stable.\n\nQuick technique notes (useful for all recipes)\n- Base: unsweetened almond or oat milk for lighter creamer; canned full\u2011fat coconut milk, cashew cream, or blended soaked cashews for a richer mouthfeel.  \n- Creaminess boost: add 1\u20132 Tbsp soaked-blended cashews or 1 Tbsp melted coconut oil per cup of thin plant milk. For very smooth texture, whisk or blend.  \n- Thickener/emulsifier (optional): 1/8\u20131/4 tsp xanthan gum per cup (blend in slowly) for a thicker \u201ccreamer\u201d feel.  \n- Sweeteners: keep low \u2014 use 1\u20132 tsp maple syrup or honey per cup, or a few drops of liquid stevia / 1/4\u20131/2 tsp monk fruit (brands vary). Taste and adjust.  \n- Protein bump: 1\u20132 scoops collagen (neutral flavor) or 1 Tbsp powdered plant protein \u2014 may change texture slightly.  \n- Storage: refrigerate in airtight jar for 5\u20137 days. Freeze in ice cube trays up to 2 months. Shake before using.\n\nFive easy recipes (all make ~1 cup; multiply as needed)\n\n1) Vanilla\u2011Almond (lighter, your base but cleaner)\n- 1 cup unsweetened almond milk  \n- 1/2 tsp pure vanilla extract  \n- 1\u20132 tsp maple syrup or honey (or stevia to taste)  \n- Pinch of fine sea salt  \nMethod: whisk or blend. Optional: 1 Tbsp cashew cream or 1/2 tsp melted coconut oil for more body.\n\n2) Creamy Coconut\u2011Vanilla (richer, still dairy\u2011free)\n- 1/2 cup full\u2011fat canned coconut milk + 1/2 cup water (or almond milk)  \n- 1/2 tsp vanilla extract  \n- 1 tsp maple syrup (or sweetener to taste)  \nMethod: blend, chill. Great if you want a \u201clatte\u201d mouthfeel without dairy.\n\n3) Cinnamon\u2011Cardamom Spice (no/low sugar)\n- 1 cup unsweetened oat or almond milk  \n- 1/2 tsp ground cinnamon + pinch ground cardamom  \n- 1/2 tsp vanilla extract  \n- 1 tsp maple or 2\u20133 drops stevia (optional)  \nMethod: warm briefly to help spice bloom, then cool and store. Lovely with espresso.\n\n4) Mocha (low sugar)\n- 1 cup unsweetened almond or oat milk  \n- 1 tsp unsweetened cocoa powder (use Dutch\u2011processed if you like)  \n- 1/2 tsp vanilla extract  \n- 1\u20132 tsp maple syrup or 1/4 tsp powdered monk fruit  \nMethod: whisk cocoa into a little warm milk first to dissolve, then blend with rest.\n\n5) Lemon\u2011Ginger Cold\u2011Brew Cremer (springy; use sparingly)\n- 1 cup unsweetened almond milk  \n- 1/2 tsp finely grated lemon zest (no juice \u2014 too acidic)  \n- 1/4 tsp fresh grated ginger or 1/2 tsp ginger syrup (for a milder flavor)  \n- 1\u20132 tsp honey or maple (optional)  \nMethod: blend and strain if you prefer no bits. Use a small amount (lemon + coffee can clash if overdone) \u2014 great in cold brew for a bright twist.\n\nCustomization ideas\n- If you want sugar reduced further, use vanilla + spices to trick the palate into feeling sweeter.  \n- For foamability (latte drinks), oat milk base foams best among non\u2011dairy options.  \n- If you dislike coconut flavor but want creaminess, cashew cream is neutral and silky.\n\nWould you like one of these written as a step\u2011by\u2011step recipe scaled for a week (e.g., quart batch) or tailored to be sugar\u2011free or high\u2011protein? Which flavor sounds best to try first?", "autoeval_label": {"model": "gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": true}}
{"question_id": "75f70248", "hypothesis": "Yes \u2014 your living room is a very plausible source. Common indoor triggers for sneezing include dust and dust mites, pet dander, mold, pollen brought in on clothing/windows, and volatile compounds from new upholstery/finishing. A couple of things from your recent history make the living room especially suspect: you said your cat sheds a lot, and you recently had that Victorian armchair reupholstered (and have been refinishing furniture), both of which can release allergens, dust and off\u2011gassing.\n\nQuick checklist you can try right away (fast, low-effort):\n- Do a short room test: spend a few hours away from the living room (or leave it closed for 24\u201348 hours). If sneezing improves, the room is likely the source.\n- Ventilate the room well (open windows when pollen counts are low) for several hours to reduce fumes/dust.\n- Wipe surfaces with a damp cloth to pick up dust rather than just dusting dry.\n- Vacuum carpets/upholstery with a HEPA or good\u2011filtration vacuum and a brush attachment (vacuum at least twice a week if cat sheds a lot).\n- Groom the cat outdoors or in a nonliving\u2011room area, and brush more often to reduce dander in the room.\n\nActions for better control:\n- Use a HEPA air purifier in the living room (target CADR appropriate for the room size).\n- Wash throw blankets, cushion covers, curtains, and removable upholstery at least every 2\u20134 weeks.\n- Replace HVAC/room\u2011fan filters with HEPA or higher MERV (if compatible) and change as recommended.\n- Reduce clutter and fabric surfaces that trap dander/dust (or use washable covers).\n- Keep indoor humidity around 30\u201350% to discourage dust mites and mold growth.\n- If you recently had furniture refinished or reupholstered, ventilate well and, if possible, avoid the room for a few days while off\u2011gassing declines.\n\nWhen to see a doctor or get testing:\n- If symptoms are persistent or severe, consider talking to your primary care doctor or an allergist for skin/blood allergy testing and treatment (antihistamines, nasal sprays, immunotherapy).\n- If you suspect mold (musty smell, water stains), have a professional check it.\n\nIf you want, I can:\n- Give a short, prioritized cleaning checklist for a single deep clean of the living room.\n- Recommend specific vacuums and air purifiers that work well for pet dander and dust.\n- Help design a simple elimination trial to figure out the exact trigger. Which would you like?", "autoeval_label": {"model": "gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": true}}
{"question_id": "1da05512", "hypothesis": "Short answer\n- If you need a central, always-on backup and easier access to files now \u2192 buy now.\n- If you can keep using your external drive and it\u2019s not urgent \u2192 you can wait for a sale or a new model, and save some money.\n\nWhy buy now\n- Immediate convenience and safety: a NAS will let you back up your laptop and phone automatically and serve files to devices without constant manual transfers.\n- Better workflow: easier media streaming, remote access (when configured securely), and centralized backups for multiple devices.\n- Beginner-friendly models are mature and easy to set up (Synology and QNAP have good UIs and guides).\n- Drives and NAS hardware aren\u2019t changing so fast that buying now will be a bad decision for typical home use.\n\nWhy you might wait\n- Sales: big discounts often hit Prime Day (summer), back-to-school (late summer), and Black Friday (Nov). Waiting for one of these can save 10\u201330% on hardware or bundle deals.\n- If you don\u2019t urgently need more storage or automation, continuing with your external backup is safe for a while.\n- If you expect to need much larger capacity or advanced features (virtual machines, heavy Plex transcoding), you might wait and research 4\u2011bay models or higher\u2011end units.\n\nPractical guidance (for a beginner)\n- 2\u2011bay vs 4\u2011bay:\n  - 2\u2011bay: simpler, cheaper, enough for most home users who want automatic backups, media streaming, and redundancy with RAID1.\n  - 4\u2011bay: choose if you want more raw capacity, plan to use RAID5/6, run VMs, host many services, or want easier future expansion.\n- Drive choice: use NAS-rated drives (WD Red Plus, Seagate IronWolf). Buy two identical drives for RAID1 in a 2\u2011bay.\n- Important: RAID is redundancy, not a backup. Keep at least one offline or offsite backup (external drive or cloud).\n- Security tips: enable automatic updates, use strong passwords, enable 2\u2011factor auth, us