aper, and a marble slab for variety.  \n- Props: dried lavender sprigs, fresh lemons and lemon slices, small wooden crates, raw cotton linen, twine, cork, vintage plates/spoons. These reinforce eco-friendly, countryside style.  \n- Small plinths, risers, and mirrored/clear acrylic sheets for different reflections and elevation.  \n- Glass jars and sample vessels if your candles use jars\u2014helps stage product with partial fills.  \n- Reusable/portable clamp system to hold delicate props in place.\n\nOptics & filters\n- Circular polarizer to control reflections on glass/jar lids.  \n- Good quality lens hood and front/rear caps.  \n- Optional protective UV or clear filter (if you normally like front-protection).\n\nColor & workflow\n- ColorChecker or X-Rite Passport for accurate color and white balance (very useful for product shots).  \n- Calibrated monitor or calibrator (Datacolor Spyder / X-Rite) if you edit color-critical images.  \n- Tethering cable and software (Capture One or Lightroom tether) \u2014 helpful for client previews and in-stand setups.\n\nCleaning & maintenance\n- Sensor blower, lens cleaning solution, microfiber cloths, LensPen, and a small sensor-cleaning kit.  \n- Padded protective case for Godox V1 (you already wanted one) and a small pouch for batteries/gels.\n\nAccessories for markets & travel\n- Comfortable camera strap (Peak Design Slide or similar) or harness for long days at fairs.  \n- Weatherproof, padded camera backpack (Peak Design Everyday, Think Tank Retrospective, or Lowepro ProTactic) \u2014 pick one sized for 1\u20132 bodies + 2\u20133 lenses + laptop.  \n- Portable folding table or tabletop backdrop support if you shoot demos at the Summer Solstice Fair.\n\nOptional lenses & specialty gear\n- Short tele-macro lens (Sony 90mm f/2.8 Macro or Tamron 90/100mm) if you want close-up texture shots of wax, wicks and labels.  \n- Extension tubes for tighter close-ups if you want to avoid buying a macro.\n\nPractical next step\nIf you want, I can:\n- Build a prioritized shopping list (budget / mid-range / pro tiers).  \n- Recommend specific models for tripods, bag and tethering gear based on your budget.  \n- Lay out a small product-shoot kit tailored to your lavender/lemon rustic style (props + backdrops + lighting plan).\n\nWhich of those categories would you like me to prioritize (budget-minded, compact kit for markets, or a full studio kit)?", "autoeval_label": {"model": "gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": true}}
{"question_id": "caf03d32", "hypothesis": "Great \u2014 here are focused, practical tips and quick fixes that will help your slow-cooker meals come out better and more reliably.\n\nTop general rules\n- Fill level: aim for about 1/2 to 2/3 full. Too empty = temperature swings; too full = undercooked center.\n- Keep the lid on. Every time you lift it you drop the temp and add 15\u201330 minutes (or more) of cook time.\n- Low vs High: low for long, gentle braises (8\u201310 hrs); high for quicker meals (4\u20136 hrs). If you have time, choose low for more tender results.\n- Safety: check internal temps with a thermometer. Poultry 165\u00b0F (74\u00b0C). For shreddable pork/beef, cook until 190\u2013205\u00b0F (88\u201396\u00b0C).\n\nPrep and flavor\n- Sear first. Brown meat and saut\u00e9 aromatics in a skillet, then deglaze with a little liquid and add to the slow cooker. That adds lots of flavor.\n- Deglaze the pan and add the liquid \u2014 don\u2019t pour browned bits down the drain.\n- Use bone-in cuts and fattier pieces for better flavor and less dryness.\n- Layer smartly: dense root veg (carrots, potatoes) on the bottom, then meat, then softer veg on top.\n\nVegetables and timing\n- Cut veg larger for long cooks so they don\u2019t turn to mush. Add quick-cooking veggies (bell pepper, peas, zucchini) in the last 30\u201360 minutes.\n- For soups/stews you prefer chunkier veg, cut them large and/or add some at the end.\n\nLiquid and consistency\n- You usually need less liquid than stovetop recipes; slow cookers don\u2019t lose much steam. Start with enough to barely cover solids if you want a stew-like result.\n- If result is too thin: remove lid and cook on high to reduce, blend some cooked veg into the sauce, or thicken with a cornstarch slurry (mix cold water + cornstarch; add and cook 10\u201320 minutes) or instant mashed potato flakes.\n- If too watery: skim fat and reduce, or thicken as above.\n\nSeasoning and finishing\n- Season lightly at the start, then adjust salt and acid at the end. Long cooking can mute salt and acidity.\n- Add delicate herbs and fresh greens near the end. Dried herbs do well added early.\n- Toast whole spices in a pan before adding for more depth.\n\nBeans and legumes\n- Canned beans are the easiest and most reliable. If using dried beans, pre-soak and boil kidney beans for 10 minutes before slow-cooking to destroy toxins (important).\n- If using dried beans without pre-soak, they may take a long time and cook unevenly.\n\nDairy and eggs\n- Add milk, cream, yogurt and cheese near the end of cooking to avoid curdling or separation.\n- For slow-cooker yogurt: heat milk to ~180\u00b0F (82\u00b0C), cool to ~110\u00b0F (43\u00b0C) before adding starter \u2014 using a thermometer helps a lot.\n\nTroubleshooting common problems\n- Meat dry/tough: likely cooked too long on high or was a lean cut. Use lower temp, add a fatty cut or a little more liquid, or switch to a different cut next time.\n- Meat undercooked: pieces were too large or not enough time/temperature. Cut into smaller chunks and check temp.\n- Vegetables mushy: add them later, cut larger, or use firmer vegetables.\n- Bland food: add salt, acid (vinegar/lemon), or fresh herbs at the end. Taste and adjust.\n- Burnt bottom: too little liquid or too high heat; try stirring early in the cook next time or use a liner sparingly (liners can change cooking dynamics).\n\nTools and technique\n- Use a good instant-read thermometer.\n- Use a metal insert if you want to sear in the same pot (safer than trying to sear in the ceramic).\n- Slow-cooker liners are convenient but can trap steam and reduce browning; don\u2019t rely on them if you need a reduction.\n- Consider a trivet to keep meat out of very starchy/acidic bottoms that can scorch.\n\nLeftovers and storage\n- Cool quickly and refrigerate within 2 hours. Freeze portions for easy meals.\n- Reheat gently; sauces often thicken after refrigeration \u2014 loosen with a bit of water/broth while reheating.\n\nQuick checklist before you start a recipe\n- Do I want tender shreddable meat? Use a fatty, bone-in cut and low/long cook.\n- Do I want chunkier veggies? Add them later or cut larger.\n- Did I brown my meat/aromatics? If not, consider at least deglazing the pan and adding those juices.\n- Is my liquid amount appropriate? Aim to cover solids or slightly less depending on stew vs soup.\n- Do I need a thermometer? Yep \u2014 it\u2019s the fastest way to avoid over/under-cooking.\n\nWould you like help with a specific recipe you\u2019ve tried (like that beef stew), or want 3 tested gluten-free slow-cooker recipes (meat, vegetarian, and a yogurt method)? I can troubleshoot a failed batch step-by-step or give recipes with exact times/temperatures.", "autoeval_label": {"model": "gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": false}}
