{"question_id": "75832dbd", "hypothesis": "Great \u2014 based on what I know of your interests (medical image analysis, explainable AI, multi\u2011modal fusion, transfer/domain adaptation, visualization/interpretability), here are targeted recommendations: top venues to follow, relevant workshops/special sessions, journals/special issues, and a short curated set of recent/high\u2011impact papers and resources you can use as a starting point. If you want, I can then pull a tailored, up\u2011to\u2011date reading list (arXiv/links) for any of these subtopics.\n\nConferences & workshops (where recent, high\u2011quality work appears)\n- MICCAI (annual): the primary venue for methodological advances in medical image analysis, segmentation, registration, and clinical translation. Check MICCAI main papers and the many workshops (XAI in Medical Imaging, Domain Adaptation, Multi\u2011modal Fusion, etc.).\n- MIDL (Medical Imaging with Deep Learning): focused exclusively on ML methods for medical imaging \u2014 good signal for reproducible, clinically relevant work.\n- ISBI (IEEE International Symposium on Biomedical Imaging): solid for both methods and applications.\n- NeurIPS / ICLR / ICML / CVPR / ECCV: top ML/CV conferences\u2014look especially at ML4Health/ML4H, medical imaging workshops, and general papers on transformers, domain adaptation, and interpretability that are applied to medical data.\n- NeurIPS/MIDL/MICCAI workshops to watch: Explainable AI / Interpretability in Medical Imaging, Domain Adaptation & Generalization in Medical Imaging, Multi\u2011modal Medical Imaging / Fusion workshops, and Robustness & Uncertainty in Medical Imaging.\n\nJournals & special issues\n- Medical Image Analysis (MedIA) \u2014 highest impact specialized journal for new methods and thorough evaluations.\n- IEEE Transactions on Medical Imaging (TMI) \u2014 rigorous methodological work and translational studies.\n- IEEE Transactions on Biomedical Engineering (TBME) \u2014 broader scope but often relevant methods.\n- npj Digital Medicine, Nature Machine Intelligence \u2014 good for high\u2011impact, multidisciplinary work and clinically\u2011oriented AI papers.\n- Check special issues (MedIA/IEEE TMI) on explainability, domain adaptation, and multi\u2011modal imaging (these pop up regularly).\n\nSelected recent papers / threads to follow (2020\u20132023; good starting points)\n- Transformers for medical imaging (architectures & segmentation)\n  - TransUNet (Chen et al., 2021) \u2014 hybrid CNN/Transformer for segmentation; kicked off heavy interest in ViT-style encoders for med\u2011image tasks.\n  - UNETR (Hatamizadeh et al., 2022) \u2014 transformer encoder directly for 3D segmentation (MR/CT), shows transformer viability for volumetric tasks.\n  - Swin\u2011based UNet variants (Swin\u2011UNet / Swin\u2011UNetR etc., 2021\u20132022) \u2014 leverage hierarchical vision transformers for better locality and scaling.\n  Why follow: these set the baseline for current state\u2011of\u2011the\u2011art architectures and influence downstream transfer/interpretability work.\n\n- Domain adaptation & robust generalization\n  - SynthSeg (Billot et al., 2020\u20132021) \u2014 domain\u2011randomized training for robust segmentation across contrasts/scanners (useful idea for reducing domain gap).\n  - Source\u2011free / test\u2011time adaptation works (2021\u20132023) \u2014 methods that adapt models at test time or without access to source data have rapidly appeared for clinical deployment scenarios.\n  Why follow: domain shift is the main barrier to clinical translation; recent papers focus on practical, deployable approaches.\n\n- Explainability & interpretability in medical imaging\n  - Surveys and critical reviews (e.g., Tjoa & Guan and follow\u2011ups, 2020\u20132022) \u2014 useful overviews of methods, limitations, evaluation metrics, and clinical considerations.\n  - Works integrating concept\u2011based explanations, counterfactuals, and uncertainty with saliency maps (2021\u20132023) \u2014 look for papers that evaluate explanations with radiologist studies or clinical tasks.\n  Why follow: XAI in med imaging needs clinically meaningful, validated explanations \u2014 this is an active area.\n\n- Multi\u2011modal fusion and multi\u2011task learning\n  - Papers that fuse image + clinical metadata (EHR, labs, reports) and multi\u2011modal imaging (e.g., PET+CT, MR sequences) \u2014 recent works leverage late/early fusion and cross\u2011modal attention (2021\u20132023).\n  - Cross\u2011modal pretraining (image + text) and contrastive learning approaches adapted to medical datasets.\n  Why follow: multi\u2011modal methods often boost clinical performance and are central for translational systems.\n\n- Visualization & interpretability techniques\n  - Gradient\u2011based and perturbation\u2011based saliency improvements, explainability evaluation benchmarks, and methods tailored to structured outputs (segmentation/volumetric outputs).\n  - Papers combining uncertainty estimation and explanations (to indicate when explanations are trustworthy).\n  Why follow: interpretability methods are improving fast \u2014 look for work with rigorous user studies.\n\nWhere to keep a live pulse of new work\n- arXiv daily (cs.CV, cs.LG, eess.IV) + filter for \u201cmedical\u201d or \u201cradiology\u201d. Set alerts for keywords: \u201cmedical imaging\u201d, \u201cdomain adaptation\u201d, \u201cexplainable\u201d, \u201ctransformer medical\u201d.\n- Twitter / X: follow MICCAI, MIDL, authors like the Hatamizadeh/Chen groups, and major labs (e.g., Google Health, NVIDIA, UNet/Transformers authors).\n- Papers with Code / OpenReview: good for reproducibility and code links.\n- Conference proceedings (MICCAI, MIDL) and workshop pages \u2014 they often list accepted papers with PDFs.\n\nPractical next step \u2014 I can:\n- Curate a targeted reading list of 10\u201315 recent (2022\u20132023) papers with links in your preferred subtopic (explainability / domain adaptation / multi\u2011modal fusion / visualization).\n- Pull together recent MICCAI/MIDL workshop proceedings on XAI and domain adaptation.\n- Assemble a short list of reproducible implementations (GitHub) for the methods above (TransUNet, UNETR, SynthSeg, etc.).\n\nWhich would you prefer? If you want the curated paper list, tell me which subtopic to prioritize (explainability, domain adaptation, multi\u2011modal fusion, or transformers/architectures) and whether you want full bibliographic entries + links and code.", "autoeval_label": {"model": "gpt-4o-2024-08-06", "label": true}}
